WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.883
- I, for one,

00:00:00.883 --> 00:00:02.640
think we should be grateful to Nvidia

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.290
for the 4660 Ti.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.123
I mean, sure,

00:00:05.123 --> 00:00:06.900
we've waited over a year for a card

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.750
that's only 10% faster
in rasterized games,

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:10.650
and sure,

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.190
they didn't upgrade the
8GBs of VRAM from last gen,

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.050
but at least they didn't downgrade it

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:17.940
like they did with the memory bus.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:18.870
And besides,

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.550
it's just $100 more

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.680
if you want the other 8GBs.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.030
That seems totally reasonable

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:24.960
when Nvidia's cost

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.050
is somewhere between $20 and $35.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.490
At least you won't have to wait months

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.290
for the drivers to get good.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:33.090
Okay, that last one
wasn't sarcasm, though.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.500
There is legitimately

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.910
a fair bit to like

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.100
about the RTX 4060 Ti.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.480
It supports all of Nvidia's

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.760
latest ray tracing and AI upscaling

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:42.810
bells and whistles.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.460
It's much more power efficient

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:45.930
than the card that it replaces,

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.060
and it supports AV1 encoding,

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:49.320
which is a huge deal

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.000
if you stream to YouTube.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.070
But you also shouldn't pull the trigger

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.200
without getting the full story here.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.910
There's been a lot of talk lately

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.560
about how 8GBs of VRAM

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.570
just isn't enough anymore,

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.550
and I've got some bad news.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.460
It's not FHD at max settings,

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.267
but only 1080p resolution.

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:08.550
"The Last of Us Remake"

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.880
gobbles up a whopping
8.9GBs of video memory,

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.190
meaning that with an 8GB card,

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:15.870
some of that is gonna spill out

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.280
into your system memory

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.590
which significantly harms performance.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:21.000
Or even worse,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.770
if you have an older system,

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.510
and not even that old,

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.970
just one that doesn't
support resizable bar,

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:28.320
there is no overflow.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.060
You simply will need to turn down

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.560
your graphical settings.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.660
And this isn't an isolated problem.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.250
We didn't test "Jedi Survivor"

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.660
for our sweep today,

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.160
but that's another recent game

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.400
that is reportedly using
up to 10GBs of VRAM

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.530
at 1080p epic settings.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:44.947
Yikes.

00:01:44.947 --> 00:01:47.257
"But Linus," you might say,

00:01:47.257 --> 00:01:49.860
"I don't need epic or ultra settings.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.960
They don't even look that much better."

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.430
And you are right.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:54.810
We've even got an entire video

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.310
about why it's kind of pointless

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.650
to turn the slider all the way to the max.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.000
But here's the thing.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.660
Even if trying to fully
futureproof is foolish,

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:04.830
I'm not talking about

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.410
some hypothetical future need here.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:11.070
If your card isn't
equipped for ultra today,

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.360
how do you think it's gonna fare

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:13.770
in two or three years

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.020
when ultra is the new medium?

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.280
Not good.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:18.420
You know what it is good, though?

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.460
Our new Sticklocks joystick covers.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:21.930
Who needs your sticks
getting knocked around

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:23.130
when your console or your controller

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:24.030
is in your bag?

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.340
Check 'em out @lttstore.com.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:27.270
With that out of the way,

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.490
if we look purely at today's performance

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.290
compared to the rest of Nvidia's lineup,

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.820
the 4060 Ti looks like
a pretty decent buy.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.030
It costs 40% less than the RTX 4070,

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.640
while being only about 20% slower,

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:42.900
except in "The Last of Us,"

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.030
probably because of that VRAM issue.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:45.990
With that said,

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:46.980
that might be more to do

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.410
with how bad the 4070 is.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:51.450
If we add some inconvenient truths

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:52.530
to our numbers here,

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:54.900
the picture gets a little less rosy.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.390
AMD has made some very
aggressive price cuts

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:58.410
to their last gen,

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.800
and the 6700 XT performs nearly as well,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.530
sometimes even beating the 4060 Ti,

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:07.500
all while costing about $50 less,

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.500
and while including 12GBs of VRAM

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.750
without $100 upcharge.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.010
So if all you care about

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:16.350
is pure rasterized performance per dollar,

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:17.250
you should probably

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.440
be making the switch to Team Red.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:20.610
But with that being said,

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.200
there is an efficiency story here

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:24.090
that we're gonna get into later.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:25.290
First, I wanna talk about

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:26.730
higher resolution gaming.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:27.870
Because even if Nvidia

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:28.980
isn't talking about it,

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.290
because only ray tracing
exists for them now,

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.493
this is a very solid 1440P

00:03:33.493 --> 00:03:35.370
rasterized gaming card.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.350
Most games had 1% lows

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:38.670
that were either close to

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.370
or above 60 FPS at ultra settings

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.890
with ray racing and DLSS off.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.570
Meaning that on a 60 hertz monitor,

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.550
you can expect a consistently smooth

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:48.990
gaming experience.

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:50.310
Want even more frames?

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.320
Hey, just enable DLSS,

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.460
or turn down your settings

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:54.780
to a more modest preset,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:56.250
and you're off to the races.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.130
1440P is just such a great middle ground.

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:00.960
It offers a much sharper image

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.160
compared to 1080p,

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:03.750
assuming the same screen size,

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:06.300
but without the same major performance hit

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:07.920
that comes with 4K.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.450
And it also comes with this message

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:10.590
from our sponsor.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:11.490
Pulseway.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.530
Managing IT systems can be a pain,

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.120
but with Pulseway's remote monitoring

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.020
and management software,

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:17.010
it's a breeze.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.510
You get real-time system monitoring,

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:19.343
instant alerts,

00:04:19.343 --> 00:04:20.760
and audio remediation,

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.050
all from one platform

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.210
at any time using any device.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:25.470
Start your free trial today

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:26.910
with the link down below.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:28.350
Speaking of 4K,

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:30.840
you can, but shouldn't.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:32.880
Some of our results were okay.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.340
And if you've got a nice 4K TV nearby,

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.260
or a secondary 4K monitor,

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:38.850
depending on the game and settings,

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.350
you could expect anywhere as high

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:43.680
as 68 FPS average in "Forza Horizon 5,"

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.690
with even ray tracing staying playable,

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.580
to as low as 11 FPS

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.620
in cranked out "Cyberpunk."

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:50.520
Games, though,

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:51.720
who needs them, right?

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:53.730
Blender loves Nvidia,

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:54.840
and if we only look

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:56.580
at this productivity benchmark,

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.290
the 4060 Ti slots in

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.102
exactly where you'd expect,

00:05:00.102 --> 00:05:02.340
but if we take a close-lier look

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:03.990
at, say, DaVinci Resolve

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.410
with AV1, H.264 and H.265,

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:08.940
well now we're losing

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:11.010
to not only several AMD cards,

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:14.340
but sometimes Intel's Arc A770 and,

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:15.660
this one hurts the most,

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:17.880
even the 3060 Ti.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:18.750
And the worst part,

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:20.430
is this isn't isolated.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.620
Digging into our SPECviewperf benchmarks,

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.010
the 6700 XT and the 3060 Ti

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.510
often come out ahead,

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.430
or basically on par,

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.500
with Nvidia's new hotness.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:32.634
But why?

00:05:32.634 --> 00:05:34.470
Okay, while I often say

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:36.810
that the specs for a GPU don't matter,

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:38.970
what matters is the performance,

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.310
sometimes the performance makes no sense

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:42.750
and we've gotta dig into the specs

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.250
to find out why.

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:47.010
The 4060 Ti is based on an AD106 Die

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:48.690
that boosts up to 2.5 gigahertz

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:50.430
with 4352 CUDA cores,

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:52.770
136 4th gen tensor cores,

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:55.350
and 34 3rd gen ray tracing cores.

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:58.050
All of which sounds sufficiently next gen.

00:05:58.050 --> 00:05:59.280
What doesn't,

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.050
is that the 8GBs of VRAM,

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:02.790
that we've talked about so much already,

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:05.790
is on a 128-bit bus.

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:07.800
Compared to the 3060 Ti,

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.300
which had double that,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.460
that is a major cost cutting measure

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.020
that seems to have significantly

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.700
harmed the 4060 Ti

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:16.920
in memory intensive applications.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.210
For context, guys,

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.220
back in the 600 series days,

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.520
you had to go all the
way down to the 650 Ti,

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:25.290
which cost $150,

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:28.560
to find such a budget-conscious
memory interface.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.510
But, to Nvidia's credit,

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:32.070
cheap bastards that they are,

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:34.290
you can't ignore how well it kept up

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:35.820
the majority of the time.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:36.960
And according to Nvidia,

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:38.130
this is largely thanks

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:41.850
to the 4060 Ti's massively upgraded cache.

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:42.840
They claim,

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:44.310
and our numbers do bear this out,

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:45.750
that it improves performance,

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.010
reduces latency,

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:49.080
and increases power efficiency.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.240
So yeah, a narrow bus

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.610
does limit data transfer speeds to VRAM,

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:54.870
and in a perfect world,

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:56.330
we wouldn't have to give that up,

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:58.740
but the more we can store in the cache

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:00.180
instead of VRAM,

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:01.230
the better.

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:02.670
Now, I'm not a GPU architect,

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.260
so I can't say this for certain,

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:05.970
but it seems like this trade-off

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.220
might be contributing
to better performance

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:09.870
in hybrid applications,

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:10.830
like gaming

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.620
while also taking
advantage of the 4060 Ti's

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:16.320
ray tracing cores and its AI engine.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:17.610
I mean, looking at performance

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:19.020
in games with ray tracing,

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:20.691
it's clear that this isn't some

00:07:20.691 --> 00:07:22.920
feature that's tacked on now.

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:24.420
It's actually usable.

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:28.140
And unless you have
$600 to blow on a 4070,

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:30.930
this is the best next
next-gen gaming experience

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:31.980
on the block.

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:32.813
And the same goes

00:07:32.813 --> 00:07:34.920
for when we turn on DLSS AI upscaling,

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.270
which, in general,

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:37.800
turns playable into smooth

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:39.960
and smooth into smooth-lier.

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:41.670
It isn't perfect.

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:43.200
There are still some visual anomalies

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.250
from time to time,

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:45.750
and we did find outliers

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:47.790
where performance did not improve.

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:50.280
For example, using DLSS 3 in "Returnal"

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:52.530
resulted in a degraded experience,

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:54.990
whether we had frame
generation enabled or not.

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:56.520
Super bizarre.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.560
But it has improved to the point

00:07:58.560 --> 00:07:59.526
where some games

00:07:59.526 --> 00:08:02.670
not only look the same and perform better,

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:05.430
but will even look better with DLSS.

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:07.406
It's definitely a nice-to-have.

00:08:07.406 --> 00:08:09.090
And what makes it even better,

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:10.350
is that the 4060 Ti

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:12.030
draws significantly less power

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:13.110
than its predecessor,

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:16.020
sometimes staying within 140 watts,

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:17.700
depending on the game or workload.

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:19.800
That is really impressive,

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:21.030
and could even be the difference

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:23.160
between needing to
upgrade your power supply

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:25.500
or not needing to upgrade
your power supply.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:26.610
This part's anecdotal,

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:28.050
we're still waiting on our sound chamber,

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:30.540
but in terms of acoustics while gaming,

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:33.360
it's no louder than really any other card,

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:34.710
which is good, I guess,

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:35.970
considering that it only reaches

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:37.590
66 degrees on average

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:39.360
during our "F1 22" loop

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.270
with a max temp of 79 on the hotspot.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:44.340
We're gonna have way more concrete testing

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:45.930
on GPU noise and thermals down the line,

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:47.100
so make sure you stay tuned

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:49.050
for more Labs updates.

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:50.280
So, then.

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:51.660
At $400,

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:54.060
is it a good product at a good price?

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:55.470
Let me put it this way.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:57.000
When Nvidia CEO, Jensen,

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.100
told all of his Pascal gamer friends

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:01.110
that it's safe to upgrade now,

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:02.250
it wasn't.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:04.740
3060s and 3060 Ti's might as well

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:06.330
have been made of unobtainium,

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:08.310
but now, honestly,

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:09.930
yeah, I agree.

00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:10.920
The only question,

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:13.830
is whether it's safe to upgrade to Nvidia.

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:16.320
The 40 series is already a mixed bag,

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.000
and now the 4060 family

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:19.560
looks like it's gonna be similarly

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:21.060
all over the map.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:25.260
At $300, the non-Ti
version could make sense,

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:26.093
but then,

00:09:26.093 --> 00:09:27.510
if these specs are anything to go by,

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:28.343
you're gonna be leaving

00:09:28.343 --> 00:09:30.270
a lot of performance on the table.

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:33.030
Then at $400, we've got good performance,

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:34.710
but do you really wanna hamstring yourself

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:36.240
with only 8GBs of RAM?

